Architecture plan for Issue #134: Fix invalid slug pattern regex in the post editor

Project shape:
- Next.js App Router app using TypeScript, React, Tailwind CSS, Prisma/PostgreSQL, and S3-compatible object storage.
- Public reader pages live under app/(public); admin CMS pages, editor components, and server actions live under app/admin.
- The post editor UI is implemented in app/admin/posts/_components/post-editor-form.tsx and submits to app/admin/posts/actions.ts.

Relevant module/file organization:
- app/admin/posts/_components/post-editor-form.tsx owns the slug input and its browser validation attributes.
- app/admin/posts/actions.ts remains the server-side source of truth for slug normalization via slugify; this issue only fixes the client-side pattern attribute.
- Existing editor upload tests live in tests/post-editor-upload-config.test.ts, but the requested change is isolated to the form markup unless validation coverage is needed.

Issue dependency analysis:
- There is one issue in this batch.
- No database, storage, authentication, API, or routing changes are required.
- No shared types or new dependencies are needed.

Key design decisions:
- Change only the slug input pattern from [a-z0-9-]+ to [a-z0-9\\-]+ so the hyphen is escaped for browser v-flag regex parsing.
- Preserve the allowed character set: lowercase letters, digits, and hyphen.
- Preserve all other slug input attributes, labels, styling, and server-side slug handling.
- Validate with the existing Node/Next toolchain; a full deployment should not require migrations or object storage changes.
